# Part 5

## 5.1 More Lists
```python
matrix = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]     # list of lists
numbers = [x**2 for x in range(5)]     # list comprehension
copy_list = numbers.copy()
numbers.insert(0, 100)
numbers.pop()                  # removes & returns last item
```

## 5.2 References
- Variables holding lists (and other mutable objects) are **references** to the same object.
```python
a = [1, 2, 3]
b = a           # b refers to the SAME list as a
b.append(4)     # a is now [1, 2, 3, 4] too!

c = a.copy()    # c is an independent copy
```

## 5.3 Dictionary
```python
person = {"name": "Ann", "age": 30}
person["email"] = "ann@example.com"   # add/update
print(person["name"])
person.get("phone", "N/A")             # safe access with default
for key, value in person.items():
    print(key, value)
"name" in person                        # membership check
```

## 5.4 Tuple
```python
point = (3, 4)
x, y = point         # unpacking
point[0]
```
- Tuples are **immutable** ordered collections; often used for fixed groups of values or multiple return values.

